Class-Level @Transactional
When you apply @Transactional at the class level, it means that all public methods within that class are transactional by default. This approach is useful when you want to ensure that every method in the service layer adheres to the same transaction management rules.
@Service
@Transactional
public class UserService {

 @Autowired
 private UserRepository userRepository;

 public void createUser(User user) {
 userRepository.save(user);
 // Additional business logic...
 }

 public User getUserById(Long id) {
 return userRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
 }
}
In this example, both createUser and getUserById methods are transactional due to the class-level @Transactionalannotation.

Method-Level @Transactional
When you apply @Transactional at the method level, only the annotated methods are transactional. This approach provides fine-grained control over transaction management, allowing you to customize transaction settings for each method individually.
@Service
public class OrderService {

 @Autowired
 private OrderRepository orderRepository;

 @Transactional
 public void placeOrder(Order order) {
 orderRepository.save(order);
 // Additional business logic...
 }

 public Order getOrderById(Long id) {
 return orderRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
 }
}
In this example, only the placeOrder method is transactional, while getOrderById is not.


